<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick V of the Palatinate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1596">1596</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Winter King · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick V was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> and the leading Calvinist prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1619 he accepted the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from the Protestant rebels against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, but his army was crushed at the White Mountain in 1620, earning him the name Winter King. He lost both Bohemia and his own lands and electoral vote, and died in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate.jpg" alt="Frederick V of the Palatinate" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_V._von_der_Pfalz_bis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 26 August 1596 at the hunting lodge in Deinschwang, the son of the Elector Frederick IV and Louise Juliana of Orange-Nassau, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>. In 1604 he was sent to Sedan, to the court of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-la-tour-dauvergne-duke-of-bouillon">Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, Duke of Bouillon</a>, where the Calvinist theologian Daniel Tilenus taught him that Protestant princes had a duty to intervene when their fellow believers were harassed.</p><p>His father died on 19 September 1610. A dispute followed over guardianship, since the nearest male relative, Wolfgang William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a>, was a Catholic and the dying elector had named John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> instead. Emperor Matthias intervened in 1613 and Frederick began personal rule before his eighteenth birthday. On 14 February 1613 he married Elizabeth Stuart, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> and I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick presented himself as a leader of the Protestant princes and head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a>, and built the Hortus Palatinus garden at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. In 1619 the Bohemian Estates, in revolt against the Catholic Ferdinand II, elected him king. He was the only elector to vote against Ferdinand's election as emperor. Historians have concluded that he acted chiefly from a sense of duty to fellow Protestants, though trade in Upper Palatinate iron and Bohemian tin and glass also counted. He was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a> on 4 November 1619.</p><p>He found a weak crown and a divided country. He and Elizabeth spoke no Czech, so court posts went mostly to foreigners. James opposed the venture and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> did not support him. He was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> on 8 November 1620 and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> the next day. Pamphleteers on both sides mocked his flight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ferdinand II declared Frederick's lands and titles forfeit in 1621. Imperial forces took the Palatinate, and an imperial edict formally deprived him of it in 1623. He lived in exile, mostly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, where he kept a government-in-exile and built a summer palace at Rhenen. In 1632 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, who would restore him only if he held the Palatinate as a Swedish fief and granted Lutherans equal rights. Frederick refused and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> on 29 November 1632.</p><p>His eldest surviving son, Charles I Louis, regained the electorate in 1648 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>. Another son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-rupert-of-the-rhine">Prince Rupert of the Rhine</a>, and his daughter Sophia was named heir presumptive to the British throne; her son became George I in 1714.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick V of the Palatinate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1607 he executed an imperial ban against the Protestant town of Donauwörth and annexed it, and after the Battle of White Mountain Emperor Ferdinand II rewarded him at Regensburg in 1623 with the electoral dignity taken from <b>Frederick V of the Palatinate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick V</b>'s acceptance of the Bohemian crown in 1619 brought catastrophe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The estates deposed Ferdinand II and elected <b>Frederick V of the Palatinate</b>, but their defeat at the Battle of White Mountain in 1620 was followed by the execution of 27 leaders in Prague’s Old Town Square in 1621, the confiscation of rebel estates and the emigration of much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bohemian estates, which had elected Habsburgs since 1526, deposed the Habsburg Ferdinand in 1619 and elected <b>Frederick V of the Palatinate</b>, the “Winter King”; after the Battle of White Mountain in 1620 the Habsburgs abolished the elective crown.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick III's descendants, including Frederick V, the <b>Winter King</b>, ruled the Electoral Palatinate until 1685.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-simmern">Palatinate-Simmern</a></li><li><blockquote>After Elector <b>Frederick V</b>'s defeat at the White Mountain in 1620, Duke Maximilian I of Bavaria occupied the Upper Palatinate and in 1628 annexed it, re-Catholicising the population.</blockquote><a href="/state/upper-palatinate">Upper Palatinate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Deinschwang, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.39,11.49&amp;year=1619" aria-label="Deinschwang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4815 442 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick V of the Palatinate"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5265 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5265 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.39,11.49&amp;year=1619">What was here in 1619? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
